//===- QDQMatMulFusion.pdll -----------------------------------------------===//
//
// PDLL pattern demonstrating declarative pattern matching.
//
// This is a simplified demonstration showing how PDLL can be used for
// pattern matching. It matches a DequantizeLinear operation and replaces
// it with hip.qmatmul.
//
// A complete implementation would:
// 1. Match the full QDQ pattern (Quantize -> MatMul -> Dequantize)
// 2. Extract scale values from the matched operations
// 3. Pass proper operands to hip.qmatmul
//
// This demonstrates the PDLL compilation workflow:
//   .pdll file --[mlir-pdll]--> .cpp.inc --[#include]--> final binary
//
// References:
// - https://mlir.llvm.org/docs/PDLL/
// - https://www.jeremykun.com/2024/08/04/mlir-pdll/
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// Simple pattern: Match any DequantizeLinear and replace with qmatmul
// This compiles successfully and demonstrates the PDLL workflow
Pattern QDQMatMulFusion =>
  replace root: Op<onnx.DequantizeLinear>
  with op<hip.qmatmul>;

//
// TODO for full implementation:
// - Match the operand chain: Quantize -> MatMul -> Dequantize
// - Extract scale parameters from onnx.Constant ops
// - Construct proper hip.qmatmul with correct operands and attributes
//
// The challenge is that PDLL's syntax for matching operand DAGs is
// different from C++. The simple version above shows that PDLL works;
// extending it requires learning PDLL's specific DAG-matching syntax.
//
